  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed breakdown of why \"pure\" gold is 99.9% and not 100%:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c 1. **True 100% Purity is Physically Impossible**\nGold is made up of atoms, and perfect purity would mean a sample has zero non-gold atoms. However, nature has its own plans, and pure gold naturally has some other atoms mixed in. In truth, it is impossible to produce 100% pure gold based on current technical limits.\n\n---\n\n### \u2697\ufe0f 2. **Limitations of the Refining Process**\nRefining metals to remove impurities costs money, and there's no benefit in making gold bullion any better than 99.99% \u2014 doing so just raises the cost with no practical benefit. It is currently impossible to refine gold to a purity of 100% (1,000 fineness). 999.999 fineness is the purest gold ever produced, and proved too expensive to be a regular occurrence.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f 3. **Trace Impurities Actually Help Stabilize Gold**\nPure gold naturally has other atoms present, and these so-called \"impurities\" actually have a job \u2014 they help stabilize the metal's structure. They are a small amount, but it makes a world of difference to the end product. Remove all the impurities, and the metal might not act like the gold we know.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccf 4. **How Gold Purity is Measured**\nGold's purity is graded on the **millesimal fineness scale**, which measures the number of parts per thousand of pure metal. Typically, anything that is 99.9% gold (999) or more is considered fine gold. The purest gold commercially available is 999.99, also called \"five nines\" fine. Just below this is 999.9 gold, also called \"four nines\" fine.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcb0 5. **99.9% is the Industry Standard for \"Pure\" Gold**\nIn the world of gold, \"pure gold\" refers specifically to 24-carat gold, which is typically labeled as 999 (99.9% pure). Generally, the closest to 100% pure gold that refiners can reach in everyday production is a fineness of 999.9. Anything at or above 999 gold is still considered pure and would fetch the same price when selling back to a dealer.\n\n---\n\n### In Summary:\nThe label \"99.9%\" (or 999 fineness) isn't a flaw or marketing trick \u2014 it's an honest reflection of the physical and chemical reality that absolute 100% purity is unachievable with any known refining technology. The tiny remaining fraction consists of trace elements that are either naturally occurring or simply too costly to remove.",
